**Finding.** `rapid-feedback.user.js:6-31` carries 19 `// @note` changelog entries (v5.0 through v5.18), ~26 header lines that duplicate what git history already records. The block only ever grows; v5.18 added another line (270fca0). No consumer: nothing in `test/` or tooling parses `@note` (grepped); Tampermonkey only acts on the structured fields (@version/@name).

**GOES.** All but the newest ~3 notes (v5.18/v5.17/v5.16) — per-user decision on N.
**STAYS.** @version, @name, @description, and the newest note(s): they are what a Tampermonkey update prompt / script editor shows.
**BREAKS (cross-examined).** Nothing at runtime — @note is free-form metadata, no reader in repo or harness. Only soft break: archaeology convenience for users reading the installed script's header; git log fully covers it.

**Decision question for owner:** keep last N notes in the header (N=2-3 suggested) and let git history hold the rest, or keep growing the block? If agreed, the todo becomes implement.
